  "summary": {
    "value": "The Ameraucana is an American breed made in the 1970s from Araucana stock that came from Chile. Breeders kept the blue egg gene but bred away the rumpless and ear tuft traits that carry lethal genes in the parent breed. The APA accepted it in 1984. Birds have a tail, a beard and muffs, a small pea comb and slate legs, in colors such as black, blue, wheaten, buff, silver and white. Large fowl roosters weigh about 6.5 pounds and hens about 5.5, and there is a bantam version too. Hens lay roughly 150 to 200 medium blue eggs a year and can be fairly broody. Many keepers find them friendly and docile, though temper varies between lines. They handle heat and cold well. Real Ameraucanas come from breeders; feed store \"Americanas\" are usually Easter Egger mixes.",
